1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,110
Each map holds five hundred and thirty-seven hits.

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:06,330
On one, every hit landed by pure chance.

3
00:00:06,710 --> 00:00:08,260
The other follows a rule.

4
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:09,940
Which one is chance?

5
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:14,790
Both are simulated, but the question is real.

6
00:00:15,170 --> 00:00:23,570
In nineteen forty-four, Londoners claimed German flying bombs fell in clusters, and precise aim would put factories at risk.

7
00:00:23,933 --> 00:00:26,203
Build the detector your eye is running.

8
00:00:26,583 --> 00:00:34,583
Cut each map into five hundred and seventy-six squares, count the hits, and flag any square with three or more, like this one.

9
00:00:34,933 --> 00:00:37,373
Map A lights up with thirty-nine targets.

10
00:00:37,753 --> 00:00:39,013
Map B gets none.

11
00:00:39,393 --> 00:00:42,193
So A looks aimed, and B looks like chance.

12
00:00:42,533 --> 00:00:43,433
Backwards.

13
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:49,983
I dropped every hit on map A at random, so all thirty-nine targets are false alarms.

14
00:00:50,363 --> 00:00:54,423
Map B's rule: no two hits closer than four fifths of a square.

15
00:00:54,803 --> 00:00:59,353
The detector, like your eye, expected chance to spread out that evenly.

16
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:00,810
Take one square.

17
00:01:01,190 --> 00:01:06,040
Each bomb has a one in five hundred and seventy-six chance of landing in it.

18
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:10,210
To stay empty, all five hundred and thirty-seven must miss.

19
00:01:10,566 --> 00:01:12,666
Guess how many squares get no hit at all.

20
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,810
About two hundred and twenty-seven squares stay empty.

21
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:20,390
Map A has two hundred and twenty-two.

22
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:30,623
Those gaps force the clumps: the other three hundred and fifty-four squares share all the hits, one and a half each, so plenty must double up.

23
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:33,196
Call the average hits per square lambda.

24
00:01:33,696 --> 00:01:40,216
One minus a small slice, raised to a big power, is almost exactly e to the minus lambda.

25
00:01:41,266 --> 00:01:48,416
For two hits, pick which two bombs land here: five thirty-seven times five thirty-six, over two, ways.

26
00:01:49,016 --> 00:01:55,726
Each lands with chance one in five seventy-six, and the rest miss: e to the minus lambda again.

27
00:01:56,666 --> 00:02:08,576
Five thirty-six is almost five thirty-seven, so for any k, each two becomes a k. The powers merge into lambda to the k: the Poisson distribution.

28
00:02:09,276 --> 00:02:14,816
Times five hundred and seventy-six squares, it predicts these purple bars.

29
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:18,116
The honest detector compares whole histograms.

30
00:02:18,496 --> 00:02:23,956
Map A sits on the purple bars: thirty-nine targets, and chance predicts thirty-nine.

31
00:02:24,336 --> 00:02:30,006
The chi-squared misfit: square each bar's gap, divide by chance's bar, and add them up.

32
00:02:30,386 --> 00:02:32,116
Map A scores two point oh.

33
00:02:32,496 --> 00:02:37,366
Pure-chance maps score worse three times in four: p equals point seven four.

34
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:45,030
Map B: half the expected empty squares, a tower of single hits, and a misfit of two hundred and seventy-two.

35
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:48,910
A pattern this even is the fingerprint of a spacing rule.

36
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:54,216
Aimed bombs would miss the other way: extra empty squares, extra pile-ups.

37
00:02:54,596 --> 00:03:06,516
In nineteen forty-six, the actuary R D Clarke published this test on real flying-bomb hits: five hundred and thirty-seven, in five seventy-six squares of south London.

38
00:03:06,866 --> 00:03:12,956
Chance predicted two hundred and twenty-seven empty squares; Clarke counted two hundred and twenty-nine.

39
00:03:13,156 --> 00:03:17,096
Single hits: two hundred and eleven, exactly as predicted.

40
00:03:17,296 --> 00:03:19,796
Same test: p equals point eight eight.

41
00:03:19,996 --> 00:03:21,466
No sign of clustering.

42
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:27,930
That doesn't mean the bombs were unaimed: they were aimed at London, just too loosely to choose a street.

43
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:37,270
And the test needs one lambda everywhere: farther north, hits fell about half as densely, so Clarke chose an area where lambda held steady.

44
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:38,823
So look again.

45
00:03:39,723 --> 00:03:43,533
The clumps in map A are what no aim at all looks like.

46
00:03:43,913 --> 00:03:47,883
If you picked map B, you picked the one following a rule.

